{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"timeless-elusive-joey","creation_channel":"cli","description":"# Context\n\nParent epic `a4bd-4ca8-c44f-49bb`. S3 makes the inbound removal path decline removals of links\nthat carry no peer-confirmation record. Without a backfill, the FIRST post-upgrade pass would\nfind an empty store and decline EVERY legitimate peer deletion — a regression far worse than the\nblind spot being fixed. The epic states the requirement directly: \"Backfill grandfathers existing\nmanaged links as confirmed-at-upgrade (marked as such) so the first post-upgrade run does not\ndecline every legitimate removal.\"\n\nThis story must land BEFORE S3 turns the decline on.\n\n# Scope\n\n- `peer_confirmations.py`: add `backfill_from_managed_refs(store, local_tickets, binding_store, pass_id)`.\n  It runs exactly once — when the store file was ABSENT at open (a fresh upgrade), not merely\n  when the store is empty (an operator who legitimately emptied it must not silently re-backfill).\n  The \"was absent\" fact is captured by S1's loader and exposed on the store.\n- For every locally-managed ref (`managed_ref_set` / `MANAGED_REF_KINDS` from\n  `src/rebar/reducer/_managed_refs.py`) whose target resolves to a BOUND peer key, write a record\n  with `source=\"backfill\"`, `direction=\"backfill\"`, `link_id=null`, `confirmed_pass=<pass_id>`,\n  `confirmed_at=<now>`. Records are explicitly MARKED as backfill so a later audit can tell\n  grandfathered evidence from proven evidence — this is a requirement, not a nicety.\n- Wire it into `reconcile.py` at the same persist-phase seam S2 uses, running before snapshot\n  confirmation so a genuine snapshot observation can upgrade a backfilled record's provenance.\n- A backfilled record is treated as confirmed by `is_confirmed()`. Distinguishing backfill from\n  proof at the DECISION point is deliberately NOT done — that would re-open the blind spot for\n  every pre-upgrade link.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] AC1 On a store whose file was absent, every managed ref with a bound target gets a record\n      with `source=\"backfill\"`; the store is then non-empty and persisted.\n- [ ] AC2 A managed ref whose target is UNBOUND gets no record (nothing to confirm against).\n- [ ] AC3 Backfill does NOT run on a store whose file existed but is empty, nor on any subsequent\n      pass once the file exists (assert: second pass adds no backfill records).\n- [ ] AC4 A ref of a kind outside `MANAGED_REF_KINDS` is not backfilled.\n- [ ] AC5 A later snapshot confirmation for a backfilled key overwrites `source` to `\"snapshot\"`\n      (provenance upgrades, never downgrades).\n- [ ] AC6 `is_confirmed()` returns True for a backfilled record.\n- [ ] AC7 Every touched module stays under the 800-line cap (verify against `git archive HEAD`);\n      `make lint` and `make typecheck` clean; new tests pass.\n\n# Out of scope\n\nThe removal decline (S3). No pruning of managed_refs (its monotonicity is unchanged).","id":"f6e9-a5fd-e908-4b82","parent_id":"a4bd-4ca8-c44f-49bb","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"story","title":"Backfill existing managed links as confirmed-at-upgrade"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1786558500749970001,"uuid":"0cb411ec-3951-4330-81e9-f888dcf175f2"}